WEBVTT

1
00:00:01.821 --> 00:00:09.926
Two thousand years ago, an Ancient Greek historian named Callimachus compiled a list of things that he thought

2
00:00:09.966 --> 00:00:12.048
were the most amazing and wondrous in the world.

3
00:00:12.588 --> 00:00:15.010
He called it "A Collection of Wonders Around the World."

4
00:00:15.730 --> 00:00:21.634
Not surprisingly, it contained a list of projects undertaken within the Greek Empire, because this was the only

5
00:00:21.694 --> 00:00:23.055
world that Callimachus knew.

6
00:00:23.756 --> 00:00:29.419
If Callimachus had traveled the world, perhaps he would have included the Great Wall of China, Stonehenge, or

7
00:00:29.459 --> 00:00:30.160
Machu Picchu.

8
00:00:31.184 --> 00:00:36.306
After the fall of the Greek and Roman empires, people forgot about their treasures for hundreds of years.

9
00:00:36.967 --> 00:00:42.409
But as the Middle Ages came to an end, people in Europe rediscovered the literature, art, and philosophy

10
00:00:42.449 --> 00:00:43.669
of ancient Greece and Rome.

11
00:00:44.350 --> 00:00:47.691
This revival became known as the Renaissance, which means "rebirth."

12
00:00:48.291 --> 00:00:53.053
During this period, the ancient Greek list of wonders was republished and became famous.

13
00:00:53.654 --> 00:00:56.875
It comprised seven buildings and statues built between 2700 and 270 BC.

14
00:00:59.868 --> 00:01:04.790
Sadly, only one of these has survived, but enough evidence remains for us to take a tour of

15
00:01:04.830 --> 00:01:06.671
the Seven Wonders of the Ancient World.

16
00:01:07.691 --> 00:01:12.753
The first Wonder of the Ancient World is the oldest, and ironically, the only ancient wonder still standing

17
00:01:12.793 --> 00:01:15.834
today: the Great Pyramid of Giza in Egypt.

18
00:01:16.595 --> 00:01:21.717
Ancient Egypt was a highly sophisticated civilization ruled by kings who were called pharaohs.

19
00:01:22.377 --> 00:01:31.121
The Great Pyramid was built between approximately 2560 and 2580 BC by the pharaoh Khufu, and, like all

20
00:01:31.161 --> 00:01:34.522
pyramids, it was both a monument to, and a tomb for, the pharaoh.

21
00:01:35.082 --> 00:01:40.765
When it was completed, the Great Pyramid was 145 meters high, and for four thousand years it was

22
00:01:40.825 --> 00:01:42.085
the world's tallest building.

23
00:01:42.705 --> 00:01:47.807
It consists of two million stone blocks, each weighing more than two tons, and was covered in a

24
00:01:47.867 --> 00:01:51.309
layer of white limestone so that it shone in the desert sun.

25
00:01:52.029 --> 00:01:58.012
Today, the mummy and treasure of Pharaoh Khufu are long gone, but the pyramid remains for modern visitors

26
00:01:58.052 --> 00:02:00.509
to enjoyㅡfive thousand years later!

27
00:02:01.490 --> 00:02:06.454
The origin of the next Wonder of the Ancient World is a lonely queen's homesickness for the familiar

28
00:02:06.494 --> 00:02:07.935
landscape of her native land.

29
00:02:08.655 --> 00:02:14.800
Nebuchadnezzar II, king of Babylon, which was located in modern-day Iraq, was married to a woman from Medea,

30
00:02:15.260 --> 00:02:16.921
now part of northwest Iran.

31
00:02:17.622 --> 00:02:21.425
The king's wife could not get used to her new home in the flat and barren plains of

32
00:02:21.545 --> 00:02:22.205
ancient Iraq.

33
00:02:22.826 --> 00:02:25.468
She longed for the lush, mountainous meadows of ancient Iran.

34
00:02:26.048 --> 00:02:29.349
To please his wife, the king built the Hanging Gardens of Babylon.

35
00:02:29.889 --> 00:02:36.211
Situated on the Euphrates River about fifty kilometers from Baghdad, the gardens took over forty years to build,

36
00:02:36.491 --> 00:02:39.212
from 604 to 562 BC.

37
00:02:39.932 --> 00:02:46.214
The Hanging Gardens were a series of terraces where exotic trees, shrubs, and flowers grew, and where even

38
00:02:46.274 --> 00:02:47.554
some rare animals lived.

39
00:02:48.194 --> 00:02:53.956
Since it rarely rains in Iraq, the gardens required an elaborate system of irrigation that pumped water from

40
00:02:53.996 --> 00:02:54.356
the river.

41
00:02:55.168 --> 00:02:59.211
While the gardens must have been an oasis in the desert, nothing remains of them today.

42
00:03:00.072 --> 00:03:04.075
The next Wonder of the Ancient World has the dual honor of being one of the largest temples

43
00:03:04.135 --> 00:03:06.837
in ancient times and of being built twice.

44
00:03:07.538 --> 00:03:11.180
The Temple of Artemis was located near modern-day Izmir in Turkey.

45
00:03:11.821 --> 00:03:17.045
It was constructed to honor the Greek goddess Artemis, the goddess of hunting, nature, and fertility.

46
00:03:17.505 --> 00:03:20.848
The temple was deemed a wonder of the world because of its size and beauty.

47
00:03:21.468 --> 00:03:27.370
It was designed by the Greek architect Chersiphron in 550 BC and was larger than the Parthenon, the

48
00:03:27.410 --> 00:03:29.650
famous Greek temple that still stands in Athens.

49
00:03:30.390 --> 00:03:37.592
With its shining white marble, 127 columns, and magnificent art and sculpture, the Temple of Artemis became a

50
00:03:37.672 --> 00:03:41.814
popular tourist attraction for local royalty, merchants, and sightseers.

51
00:03:42.434 --> 00:03:49.095
In 356 BC, the temple was destroyed by fire when a man named Herostratus wanted to immortalize himself

52
00:03:49.136 --> 00:03:50.676
by ruining this great edifice.

53
00:03:51.328 --> 00:03:54.890
The temple was rebuilt, but was later destroyed by fire once again.

54
00:03:55.811 --> 00:03:59.273
The fourth Wonder of the Ancient World is associated with the Olympic Games.

55
00:03:59.873 --> 00:04:03.916
Did you know that the first Olympic Games were held in ancient Greece more than two thousand years

56
00:04:03.976 --> 00:04:10.380
ago? The ancient Olympic Games were a celebration of both sport and religion, so a temple was included

57
00:04:10.440 --> 00:04:12.301
at the site of the games in Olympia, Greece.

58
00:04:13.021 --> 00:04:17.424
As the games grew more popular, a statue to honor the Greek god Zeus was commissioned.

59
00:04:17.996 --> 00:04:24.259
The sculptor Pheidias built a four-story-high statue of Zeus, using wood for the god's body, and pieces of

60
00:04:24.459 --> 00:04:26.280
ivory placed on top for his skin.

61
00:04:26.900 --> 00:04:31.822
The sandals and robe were made of solid gold, and the throne was decorated with precious gems and

62
00:04:31.942 --> 00:04:35.564
ivory. It was said to be the greatest Greek sculpture ever made.

63
00:04:36.104 --> 00:04:42.767
After standing for over eight hundred years at Olympia, it was taken to Constantinople (modern-day Istanbul), where it

64
00:04:42.827 --> 00:04:44.308
was later destroyed by fire.

65
00:04:45.280 --> 00:04:50.524
The next Wonder of the Ancient World is the Mausoleum at Halicarnassus, and like the Great Pyramid at

66
00:04:50.544 --> 00:04:52.305
Giza, it was a royal tomb.

67
00:04:52.945 --> 00:04:57.949
This enormous white marble tomb was built to hold the remains of Mausolus and his wife, Artemisia.

68
00:04:58.689 --> 00:05:01.371
Mausolus was a provincial king in the Persian Empire.

69
00:05:01.952 --> 00:05:06.395
The tomb was built in 350 BC on a hill that now overlooks the city of Bodrum in

70
00:05:06.435 --> 00:05:07.255
modern-day Turkey.

71
00:05:07.916 --> 00:05:13.620
Greek architects designed the forty-meter-high tomb, and four famous Greek sculptors decorated its walls.

72
00:05:14.288 --> 00:05:19.671
On top were massive, carved stone figures of four horses pulling a chariot in which statues of the

73
00:05:19.751 --> 00:05:20.872
king and queen rode.

74
00:05:21.492 --> 00:05:26.455
The tomb was so enormous that everyone who saw it was amazed by its grandeur, and the word

75
00:05:26.495 --> 00:05:30.477
"mausoleum" became the Greek, and later the English, word for a large tomb.

76
00:05:31.377 --> 00:05:36.480
The next Wonder of the Ancient World was a statue with great political, as well as artistic, significance.

77
00:05:37.021 --> 00:05:41.203
The Colossus of Rhodes was a statue built at the harbor of the city of Rhodes, on the

78
00:05:41.283 --> 00:05:42.944
island of Rhodes in ancient Greece.

79
00:05:43.616 --> 00:05:50.118
"Colossus" in Greek means "gigantic statue," and from this word comes the English word "colossal," meaning "enormous."

80
00:05:50.818 --> 00:05:55.639
The huge statue was erected after the city of Rhodes fought off a siege from the nearby country

81
00:05:55.859 --> 00:05:56.660
of Macedonia.

82
00:05:57.160 --> 00:06:02.261
The Greek sculptor Chares and the people in his workshop worked twelve years to build a bronze statue

83
00:06:02.301 --> 00:06:03.821
in honor of the sun god Helios.

84
00:06:04.662 --> 00:06:09.863
At approximately 36 meters high, it stood almost as high as the Statue of Liberty in the United

85
00:06:09.923 --> 00:06:15.513
States. Helios was posed in a traditional Greek manner: nude and wearing a spiky crown.

86
00:06:16.113 --> 00:06:21.454
Just 56 years after it was built, a strong earthquake shook Rhodes and the colossus broke at its

87
00:06:21.494 --> 00:06:23.935
weakest pointㅡthe kneesㅡand collapsed.

88
00:06:24.935 --> 00:06:29.697
The most recent Wonder of the Ancient World is the Lighthouse of Alexandria, the first lighthouse in the

89
00:06:29.737 --> 00:06:31.457
world and perhaps the most innovative.

90
00:06:31.957 --> 00:06:35.198
It is the only ancient wonder that also had a practical purpose!

91
00:06:35.739 --> 00:06:40.560
The lighthouse was situated on the island of Pharos in the harbor of the Egyptian city of Alexandria.

92
00:06:41.148 --> 00:06:46.771
Ancient Alexandria was a busy port with dangerous sailing conditions, and so a lighthouse was constructed there in

93
00:06:46.891 --> 00:06:47.712
270 BC.

94
00:06:48.292 --> 00:06:51.614
Modern lighthouses use a massive beam of light powered by electricity.

95
00:06:52.194 --> 00:06:57.477
This ancient lighthouse used curved mirrors to reflect the sun's rays during the day, and the light of

96
00:06:57.537 --> 00:06:58.618
a fire during the night.

97
00:06:59.238 --> 00:07:01.940
Its light could be seen more than fifty kilometers out to sea.

98
00:07:02.420 --> 00:07:07.963
The lighthouse stood for 1,500 years, but after years of earthquakes, it collapsed in 1326 AD.

99
00:07:10.208 --> 00:07:12.389
Where are the Wonders of the Ancient World today?

100
00:07:12.989 --> 00:07:18.532
The unpredictable and powerful forces of nature, as well as the actions of humans, have destroyed all the

101
00:07:18.652 --> 00:07:22.913
ancient wonders except for the Great Pyramid of Giza, which still stands today.

102
00:07:23.474 --> 00:07:25.054
However, there is some good news!

103
00:07:25.534 --> 00:07:29.416
The British Museum has parts of the Mausoleum of Halicarnassus on display.

104
00:07:29.936 --> 00:07:34.318
The ruins of the Temple of Artemis and the temple which held the statue of Zeus still lie

105
00:07:34.338 --> 00:07:36.819
in the grass and rubble where they once stood in Greece.

106
00:07:37.660 --> 00:07:44.003
Archaeologists are investigating whether the Hanging Gardens of Babylon were fact or myth, and while the Colossus of

107
00:07:44.083 --> 00:07:50.107
Rhodes was completely destroyed, it was the inspiration for the United States' most famous attraction, the Statue of

108
00:07:50.147 --> 00:07:55.510
Liberty. Finally, to compensate for the disappearance of the Wonders of the Ancient World, a list of modern

109
00:07:55.571 --> 00:07:59.133
wonders was compiled, and you guessed itㅡall of them are standing!
